st be nilpotent as well (Conjecture 2).\nTitle: A conjecture related to the nilpotency of groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs\nAuthors: Valentina Grazian, Carmine Monetta\nDate: Fri Feb  3 14:28:26 2023\n\nIn this work we discuss whether the non-commuting graph of a finite group can determine its nilpotency. More precisely, Abdollahi, Akbari and Maimani conjectured that if $G$ and $H$ are finite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs and $G$ is nilpotent, then $H$ must be nilpotent as well (Conjecture 2). We pose a new conjecture (Conjecture 3) that, together with the assumption $|Z(G)|\\geq|Z(H)|$, implies Conjecture 2 and we prove it for groups in which all centralizers of non-central elements are abelian.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumerative combinatorics, (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/10179955/1/BishalDeb_PhDThesis_Final.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Sokal and Zeng stated the following conjecture in their paper: ... Sokal and Zeng [SZ22, Theorem 2.4] proved this continued fraction subject to\n5.1. Introduction\n192\nSokal and Zeng stated the following conjecture in their paper:\nConjecture 5.1.1 ([SZ22, Conjecture 2.3]). The ordinary generating function of the\npolynomials bQn specialised to v1 = y1 has the J-type continued fraction\n∞\n∑\nn=0\nbQn(x1,x2,y1,y2,u1,u2,y1,v2,w,λ)tn =\n1\n1−λw0t −\nλx1y1t2\n1−(x2+y2+λw1)t −\n(λ +1)(x1+u1)y1t2\n1−(x2+y2+u2+v2+λw2)t −\n(λ +2)(x1+2u1)y1t2\n1−···\n(5.6)\nwith coefﬁcients\nγ0\n=\nλw0\n(5.7a)\nγn\n=\n[x2+(n−1)u2]+[y2+(n−1)v2]+λwn\nfor n ≥1\n(5.7b)\nβn\n=\n(λ +n−1)[x1+(n−1)u1]y1\n(5.7c)\nSokal and Zeng [SZ22, Theorem 2.4] proved this continued fraction subject to\nthe further specialisation v2 = y2 using the Biane bijection. Here we will prove the\nfull conjecture by using the Foata–Zeilberger bijection, suitably reinterpreted.\nIn Section 5.2, we will see that this conjecture is a special case of a more general\nJ-fraction involving ﬁve families of inﬁnitely many indeterminates and one additional\nvariable. We will prove these results in Section 5.4.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGrazian–Monetta nilpotency conjecture for non-commuting graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/357590/grazian-monetta-nilpotency-conjecture-for-non-commuting-grap)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Grazian–Monetta nilpotency conjecture for non-commuting graphs ... This conjecture concerns the remaining case of determining whether a group with a non-commuting graph isomorphic to that of a finite nilpotent group must itself be nilpotent, under the additional condition on the orders of the centers; the source gives no resolution.\n\n# Grazian–Monetta nilpotency conjecture for non-commuting graphs\n\nAbout 3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet $p$p be a prime, and let $G=P\\times A$G=P×A be a finite group where $P\\in\\operatorname{Syl}_p(G)$P∈Syl p​(G) is non-abelian and $A$A is an abelian $p'$p′-group. Let $H$H be a finite group whose non-commuting graph is isomorphic to that of $G$G, and suppose that $|Z(G)|\\geq |Z(H)|$∣Z(G)∣≥∣Z(H)∣. Grazian–Monetta conjecture. Then there is a prime $q$q such that\n\n$$H=Q\\times B,$$H=Q×B,\n\nwhere $Q\\in\\operatorname{Syl}_q(H)$Q∈Syl q​(H) is non-abelian and $B$B is an abelian $q'$q′-group. In particular, $H$H is nilpotent. This conjecture concerns the remaining case of determining whether a group with a non-commuting graph isomorphic to that of a finite nilpotent group must itself be nilpotent, under the additional condition on the orders of the centers; the source gives no resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nValentina Grazian, Andrea Lucchini and Carmine Monetta, “Group nilpotency from a graph point of view”, arXiv:2303.01093 (2023).\n\nfinite-groupsgraph-theorynilpotent-groups\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature 