3) 389–402\n|CH(k)| = |K| = c|Z(H)|\nand\n|CH(xi)| = qti|Z(H)|\nfor distinct integers ti ≥1. Note that from (c, q) = 1 we see that |CH(k)| ̸= |CH(xi)|.\nAiming for a contradiction, suppose there exists an isomorphism of graphs φ: ΓH →\nΓG. Let g = Φ(k), M = CG(g) and yi = φ(xi) for every 1 ≤i ≤v. Note that G/Z(G) is a \np-group, so we can set [M : Z(G)] = pm for some integer m ≥1 and [CG(yi): Z(G)] = psi\nfor some distinct integers si ≥1. Also, |K| −|CH(xi)| = |M| −|CG(yi)|, so |M| ̸= |CG(yi)|, \nthat is, m ̸= si for every i ≥1.\nClaim 1: p ̸= q, so part (1) holds.\nProof: Since ΓH ∼= ΓG we have\n|H| −|K| = |G| −|M| =⇒qubc(qf −1) = pm+ra(pn−m −1)\n(5)\nand\n|CH(xi)| −|Z(H)| = |CG(yi)| −|Z(G)| =⇒qub(qti −1) = pra(psi −1).\n(6)\nAiming for a contradiction, suppose p = q. Then (p, b) = (p, c) = (p, a) = 1. \nFrom Equation (5) we deduce that pu = pm+r and from Equation (6) we get \npu = pr, a contradiction. Therefore p ̸= q.\nClaim 2: for every d ≤r we have that pd divides bc if and only if pd divides b.\nProof: By assumption pd divides pr and by Claim 1 we have p ̸= q. We conclude \nby the following equality:\n|K| −|Z(H)| = |M| −|Z(G)| =⇒qu(bc −b) = pra(pm −1)\n(7)\nClaim 3: pr+1 does not divide bc and qu+1 does not divide a. In particular (p, c) = 1\nand so (p, [H : Z(H)]) = 1.\nProof: Recall that |K| −|CH(xi)| ̸= 0 since c does not divide q, and so |M| ̸=\n|Cg(y)|. Note that\n|K| −|CH(xi)| = |M| −|CG(yi)| =⇒qu(bc −bqti) = pra(pm −psi).\n(8)\nFrom Claim 1 we know p ̸= q and by Equation (8) we get that pr+1 di-\nvides (bc −bqti). Aiming for a contradiction, suppose pr+1 divides bc. Then pr+1\nmust divide b and by Equation (7) we deduce that pr+1 divides pra(pm −1), a \ncontradiction. Therefore pr+1 does not divide bc.\nSimilarly, if qu+1 divides a, then Equation (8) implies that q divides bc, reach-\ning again a contradiction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlip Distance of Non-Crossing Spanning Trees: NP-Hardness and Improved Bounds (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.22262)\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: Flip Distance of Non-Crossing Spanning Trees: NP-Hardness and Improved Bounds ... Date: Mon Mar 23 17:48:50 2026 ... For a set $P$ of $n$ points in general position in the plane, the flip graph $F(P)$ has a vertex for each non-crossing spanning tree on $P$ and an edge between any two spanning trees that can be transformed into each other by the exchange of a single edge.\nTitle: Flip Distance of Non-Crossing Spanning Trees: NP-Hardness and Improved Bounds\nAuthors: Håvard Bakke Bjerkevik, Joseph Dorfer, Linda Kleist, Torsten Ueckerdt, Birgit Vogtenhuber\nDate: Mon Mar 23 17:48:50 2026\n\nWe consider the problem of reconfiguring non-crossing spanning trees on point sets. For a set $P$ of $n$ points in general position in the plane, the flip graph $F(P)$ has a vertex for each non-crossing spanning tree on $P$ and an edge between any two spanning trees that can be transformed into each other by the exchange of a single edge. This flip graph has been intensively studied, lately with an emphasis on determining its diameter diam$(F(P))$ for sets $P$ of $n$ points in convex position. The current best bounds are $\\frac{14}{9}n-O(1) \\leq$ diam$(F(P))<\\frac{15}{9}n-3$ [Bjerkevik, Kleist, Ueckerdt, and Vogtenhuber; SODA 2025]. The crucial tool for both the upper and lower bound are so-called *conflict graphs*, which the authors stated might be the key ingredient for determining the diameter (up to lower-order terms). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs have the same nilpotency property - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S002186932300618X)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; We prove that if G and H are non-abelian groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs, such that G is nilpotent, then H is nilpotent, provided $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$. ... Grazian and C. ... Monetta [5], proved the conjecture when G is an AC-group and $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$, where G is an AC-group if every centralizer of non-central elements of G are abelian. ... [5, Theorem A] Let p be a prime and let $G=P\\times A$ be a finite non-abelian nilpotent AC-group, where $P\\in Sy{l}_{p}(G)$ is non-abelian and A is an abelian ${p}^{\\prime }$-group.\n\n## Journal of Algebra\n\nDate: 15 March 2024\n\nPages: 60-64\n\nVolume: Volume 642\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nGet rights and content\n\nResearch Paper\n\nFinite groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs have the same nilpotency property☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Hamid Shahverdi\n\nShow more\n\n10.1016/j.jalgebra.2023.12.007\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet G be a non-abelian group and $Z(G)$ be the center of G. The non-commuting graph ${\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{G}$ associated to G is the graph whose vertex set is $G\\setminus Z(G)$ and two distinct elements $x,y$ are adjacent if and only if $xy\\ne yx$. We prove that if G and H are non-abelian groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs, such that G is nilpotent, then H is nilpotent, provided $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$. Actually we prove conjecture 3 proposed in V. Grazian and C. Monetta (2023) [5].\n\nRecently V. Grazian and C. Monetta [5], proved the conjecture when G is an AC-group and $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$, where G is an AC-group if every centralizer of non-central elements of G are abelian. We prove the assumption AC-group in the recent article [5] could be omitted. Actually we prove:\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\n[5, Conjecture 3] Let p be a prime and suppose $G=P\\times A$ is a finite group where $P\\in Sy{l}_{p}(G)$ is non-abelian and A is an abelian ${p}^{\\prime }$-group. If ${\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{G}\\cong {\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{H}$ for a finite group H and $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$ then $H=Q\\times B$, where q is a prime, $Q\\in Sy{l}_{q}(H)$ is non-abelian and B is an abelian ${q}^{\\prime }$-group. In particular, H is nilpotent.\n\nNow above, Theorem 2.4 of [3] and Theorem 1.2 of [1] give\n\nCorollary 1.4\n\nLet G and H be two finite non-abelian groups such that ${\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{G}\\cong {\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{H}$. If G is nilpotent, then H is nilpotent, provided $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$.\n\nThe above Corollary also answers Problem 16.1 of Kourovka Notebook [7] with additional assumption $|Z(G)|\\ge |Z(H)|$. Towards formulating Conjecture 3 of [5], authors proved the following reduction theorem.\n\nTheorem 1.5\n\n[5, Theorem A] Let p be a prime and let $G=P\\times A$ be a finite non-abelian nilpotent AC-group, where $P\\in Sy{l}_{p}(G)$ is non-abelian and A is an abelian ${p}^{\\prime }$-group. Let H be a group such that ${\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{G}\\cong {\\mathrm{\\Gamma }}_{H}$. Then H is a finite AC-group and either\n\n  * (1)\n\n$H=Q\\times B$, where q is a prime, $Q\\in Sy{l}_{q}(H)$ is non-abelian and B is an abelian ${q}^{\\prime }$-group; or\n\n  * (2)\n\n$|Z(H)|\\ge |Z(G)|$, $|P:Z(P)|\\ge {p}^{4}$, none of the maximal subgroups of G is abelian (so P does not have maximal nilpotency class) and $H/Z(H)$ is a Frobenius group with Frobenius kernel and complement $F/Z(H)$ and $K/Z(H)$, respectively, where K is an abelian subgroup of H, $Z(F)=Z(H)$, and $|F:Z(H)|={q}^{t}$ for some prime $q\\ne p$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructural Properties of Shortest Flip Sequences Between Plane Spanning Trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05205)\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Date: Thu Mar  5 14:16:16 2026\n\nWe study the reconfiguration of plane spanning trees on point sets in the plane in convex position, where a reconfiguration step (flip) replaces one edge with another, yielding again a plane spanning tree. ... The folklore happy edge conjecture suggests that any edge shared by both the initial and target tree is never flipped in a shortest flip sequence.\nTitle: Structural Properties of Shortest Flip Sequences Between Plane Spanning Trees\nAuthors: Oswin Aichholzer, Joseph Dorfer, Peter Kramer, Christian Rieck, Birgit Vogtenhuber\nDate: Thu Mar  5 14:16:16 2026\n\nWe study the reconfiguration of plane spanning trees on point sets in the plane in convex position, where a reconfiguration step (flip) replaces one edge with another, yielding again a plane spanning tree. The flip distance between two trees is then the minimum number of flips needed to transform one tree into the other. We study structural properties of shortest flip sequences. The folklore happy edge conjecture suggests that any edge shared by both the initial and target tree is never flipped in a shortest flip sequence. The more recent parking edge conjecture, which would have implied the happy edge conjecture, states that there exist shortest flip sequences which use only edges of the start and target tree, and edges in the convex hull of the point set. Finally, another conjecture that is implicit in the literature is the reparking conjecture which states that no edge is flipped more than twice. Essentially all recent flip algorithms respect these three conjectures and the properties they imply. We study cases in which the latter two conjectures hold and disprove them for the general setting. (Shortened abstract due to arXiv restrictions.)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCarmine MONETTA | Pubblicazioni (https://docenti.unisa.it/028874/ricerca/pubblicazioni?anno=0)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ; Monetta, C. ... #### Neighborhoods, connectivity, and diameter of the nilpotent graph of a finite group ...  | Grazian, V. ... #### A conjecture related to the nilpotency of groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs\n#### A conjecture related to the nilpotency of groups with isomorphic non-commuting graphs